= Exploratory Data Analysis

== Exploring Across Potential Variables

Distributions of 15 initially considered parameters were examined for the 54,745 sampled crashes in 2020. The overview figures describe the sample before the selected variables are examined in relation to injury severity. The percentages shown in these overview figures are unweighted.

#figure(
  image("figures/figure1_outcomes_participants.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Injury outcomes and people involved in the 2020 crash sample. The five panels show maximum injury severity, number of injuries, number of vehicles, number of pedestrians, and youngest driver age. Percentages and counts are unweighted.],
) <fig-overview-outcomes>

The first overview figure shows that 44.2% of sampled crashes had no apparent injury and 61.0% involved two vehicles. Most crashes had no pedestrian involved. Youngest driver age is calculated from driver records for each crash; crashes without a known driver age are omitted from that histogram.

#figure(
  image("figures/figure2_conditions_geography.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Weather, lighting, crash hour, urban or rural classification, and geographic region in the 2020 crash sample. Percentages and counts are unweighted.],
) <fig-overview-conditions>

The second overview figure shows that clear weather and daylight were the most common recorded conditions. Sample crash counts were highest around 5 PM. These counts describe when and where sampled crashes occurred; they do not measure crash risk per mile traveled.

#figure(
  image("figures/figure3_behavior_context.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Alcohol involvement, work-zone involvement, collision type, speeding involvement, and within-crash seat-belt use in the 2020 crash sample. Percentages and counts are unweighted.],
) <fig-overview-context>

The third overview figure summarizes crash circumstances and safety behavior. Speeding is based on whether any vehicle in the crash was coded as speeding. The seat-belt panel shows the proportion of eligible occupants with known belt status who used a belt; crashes without a known eligible occupant are omitted from that panel.

== Selection of Variables

The initial set included 15 parameters: injury severity, number of injuries, alcohol involvement, weather conditions, lighting conditions, number of vehicles, crash timing, urban or rural location, geographic region, work-zone involvement, collision type, speeding involvement, driver age, number of pedestrians, and seat belt use.

For the focused exploratory data analysis (EDA), eight parameters were retained: injury severity, number of injuries, alcohol involvement, weather conditions, lighting conditions, number of vehicles, crash timing, and urban or rural location. The selection was based on relevance to crash conditions and injury outcomes, rather than a completed statistical feature-selection procedure.

The selected parameters were organized into two categories. The first focuses on environmental, temporal, and location factors, including weather, lighting, crash timing, and urban or rural location. The second focuses on crash characteristics and injury outcomes, including alcohol involvement, number of vehicles, and number of injuries. Maximum injury severity (`MAXSEV_IM`) was used as the common outcome. Number of injuries (`NO_INJ_IM`) was examined descriptively but was excluded from prediction inputs because it is another crash outcome.

== Environmental, Temporal, and Location Factors

For severity comparisons, 54,544 records with maximum injury severity coded from 0 to 4 were retained. CRSS survey weights were applied to the percentages reported in this subsection.

Approximately 3.64% of weighted crashes involved serious or fatal injuries. The variation across crash conditions was examined to identify candidate predictors.

=== Temporal Distribution of Crashes

#figure(
  image("figures/timing_heatmap.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Hourly distribution of crashes across different days of the week in 2020. Values are survey-weighted and normalized within each day.],
)

Crash frequency varies by hour and day of the week, with the highest weighted totals observed in the afternoon, particularly between 3 PM and 5 PM. These patterns describe the timing of reported crashes, not exposure-adjusted crash risk or injury severity. A separate severity comparison would be needed to establish whether crash timing is useful for predicting injury outcomes.

=== Weather Conditions and Injury Severity

#figure(
  image("figures/severity_by_weather.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Weighted distribution of maximum injury severity under different weather conditions in 2020.],
)

The weighted proportion of serious or fatal injuries was approximately 3.92% for crashes recorded under clear conditions and 2.60% under rainy conditions. This does not establish that clear weather is more dangerous. Differences in traffic exposure, travel speeds, and driving behavior may influence the observed proportions. Weather was therefore retained as a candidate predictor rather than interpreted as an independent cause of injury severity.

=== Lighting Conditions and Injury Severity

#figure(
  image("figures/severity_by_lighting.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Weighted distribution of maximum injury severity under different lighting conditions in 2020.],
)

Serious or fatal injuries accounted for approximately 3.02% of weighted daylight crashes, 4.58% of crashes in lighted darkness, and 5.55% of crashes in unlighted darkness. The proportion in unlighted darkness was approximately 1.8 times that during daylight. Lighting may therefore be informative for severity classification, although its association with time, roadway type, and traffic conditions should also be considered.



=== Urban and Rural Locations

#figure(
  image("figures/severity_by_urbanicity.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Weighted distribution of maximum injury severity in urban and rural areas in 2020.],
)

Serious or fatal injuries accounted for approximately 4.13% of weighted rural crashes and 3.46% of weighted urban crashes. The difference may be related to roadway type, travel speed, or other crash conditions. Urban or rural classification was retained as a predictor, without treating the observed association as causal.

== Crash Characteristics

Alcohol involvement and the number of vehicles were examined as additional crash characteristics. The number of injuries was also summarized as an outcome associated with severity.

=== Alcohol Involvement and Injury Severity

The figure compares the distribution of injury severity within crashes recorded with and without alcohol involvement. The percentages are conditional on each alcohol category and therefore allow the outcome distributions to be compared.

#figure(
  image("figures/alc_vs_severity_rev.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Injury severity distribution by alcohol involvement in 2020.],
)

Among crashes without reported alcohol involvement, 45.48% were classified as having no apparent injury, 10.79% as suspected serious injury, and 2.07% as fatal injury. For alcohol-involved crashes, suspected serious injuries accounted for 23.95%, compared with 10.79% among crashes without alcohol involvement. The fatal-injury proportion was also approximately four times higher in the alcohol-involved group. These descriptive results indicate an association between alcohol involvement and more severe outcomes; they do not establish causation.

=== Number of Vehicles and Injury Severity

The heatmap summarizes crash counts across vehicle-count and severity categories. One- and two-vehicle crashes account for the largest shares of the sample, particularly among crashes with no apparent or possible injury.

#figure(
  image("figures/total_vehicle_vs_severity_rev.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Injury severity distribution by total vehicles involved in 2020.],
)

Serious and fatal injury categories are also concentrated among crashes involving one or two vehicles. However, this largely reflects the frequency of these crashes. The plot does not by itself show whether an individual one-vehicle crash has a higher probability of severe injury than a crash involving more vehicles.

=== Number of Injuries and Injury Severity

The distribution of the number of injured persons was examined within each recorded severity category. This comparison describes the relationship between two crash outcomes and was not used to select a prediction input.

#figure(
  image("figures/numinj_vs_severity_rev.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Injury severity distribution by number of injuries in 2020.],
)

Crashes with no apparent injury were concentrated in the zero-injury category. Among crashes with recorded injuries, one injured person was the most common count. Multiple injuries accounted for larger shares in some serious and fatal outcome categories. Because the number injured is directly related to crash consequences, it was excluded from model inputs to avoid target leakage.

== Relationships and Implications for Prediction

The EDA identified differences in severity distributions associated with alcohol involvement, lighting, location, and other crash characteristics. The hourly patterns were informative about crash frequency but did not directly establish differences in severity. These findings supported the initial choice of six predictor groups: weather, lighting, time, urban or rural location, alcohol involvement, and vehicle count.

The selected predictors may also be related to each other. For example, lighting varies with time, and environmental conditions may affect driving behavior. The descriptive comparisons do not measure the independent effect of any factor. The following modeling section therefore examines how well the available variables classify injury outcomes, while excluding the number of injuries to avoid target leakage. Results based on unweighted model training are not equivalent to national CRSS population estimates.

= Predictive Modeling

== Preliminary Modeling Approach

Three approaches were investigated: logistic regression, manually specified decision rules, and multilayer neural networks. These methods were applied to different injury-classification tasks. Logistic regression focused on serious or fatal injury versus other outcomes, the rule-based models on any injury versus no apparent injury, and the neural networks on fatal versus nonfatal injury or the full five-level severity classification. The different targets were chosen to examine distinct safety questions. Any-injury classification measures the presence of harm, serious/fatal classification focuses on substantial injuries, fatal-only classification examines the rarest outcome, and five-class classification retains more detailed injury information.

Because these tasks have different class frequencies and were evaluated with different test splits, their accuracy values are not directly comparable. Comparisons are made within each task, and the overall findings are interpreted as preliminary evidence rather than as a ranking of the three approaches.

== Logistic Regression-Based Classification

=== Objective and Data Preparation

Logistic regression was used to classify crashes as serious or fatal injury (codes 3 and 4) versus all other included severity levels. This task was selected to examine whether the available predictors distinguish crashes with more severe outcomes. The severe-injury group represented 14.45% of the unweighted sample. Six predictor groups were encoded as 20 features: weather, lighting, time of day, urban or rural location, alcohol involvement, and vehicle count. The number of injured persons was excluded because it is a crash outcome.

Of the 54,544 eligible crashes, 34,908 (64%) were assigned to training, 8,727 (16%) to validation, and 10,909 (20%) to testing. Model settings and the classification threshold were selected using the validation data; the test set was reserved for final evaluation. This split differs from the splits used in the other modeling sections.

=== Training and Validation

The model was implemented in Julia as a single linear layer with a sigmoid function and trained for 3,000 steps using Zygote. The logistic regression output represents the model's estimated probability of the positive severity category. A learning rate of 0.20 led to faster convergence than 0.05, with training loss approaching 0.402. Standardizing the numerical inputs produced nearly unchanged validation performance (receiver operating characteristic area under the curve, ROC-AUC, 0.6533; precision-recall area under the curve, PR-AUC, 0.2629).

#figure(
  image("figures/fig1_logistic_convergence.png", width: 85%),
  caption: [Logistic Regression Training Convergence. A learning rate of 0.20 learns faster than 0.05 and settles at a steady training error of ~0.402.]
) <fig-lr-convergence>

Five-fold cross-validation was performed on the training records. The mean ROC-AUC was 0.6276 ± 0.0095, and the mean PR-AUC was 0.2453 ± 0.0112. The relatively small variation across folds indicates consistent ranking performance for these training subsets, but does not establish that the predictions are sufficiently accurate for practical use.

Regularization was also examined. An L2 penalty of 0.01 reduced validation ROC-AUC from 0.6500 to 0.6419. An L1 penalty of 0.001 retained seven nonzero features with a validation ROC-AUC of 0.6481. The unregularized model provided the highest tested validation ROC-AUC and was retained for the final evaluation.

=== Test Results and Classification Threshold

The test ROC-AUC was 0.6186, and the test PR-AUC was 0.2241. The latter exceeded the positive-class prevalence of 0.1363 in the test set. However, with the default threshold of 0.50, every test crash was classified as non-severe. This produced 86.37% accuracy but zero recall for serious or fatal crashes. Accuracy alone was therefore not an adequate measure of performance.

#figure(
  image("figures/fig2_cv_and_pr.png", width: 95%),
  caption: [Cross-Validation ROC Curves (Left) and Final Test Precision-Recall Curve (Right). All five training folds cluster tightly around the average (0.628 ± 0.010), and test PR-AUC (0.224) beats the 0.136 majority baseline.]
) <fig-lr-cv-pr>

The classification threshold was adjusted using the validation set. A threshold of 0.125 produced the highest validation F1 score, where F1 combines precision and recall. On the test set, this threshold gave 57.71% accuracy, 18.99% precision, 64.36% recall, and F1 = 0.2932. This means 957 of the 1,487 serious or fatal crashes were identified, with 4,083 non-severe crashes incorrectly classified as severe. A class-weighted alternative at threshold 0.50 achieved 68.45% accuracy and 43.58% recall. The threshold choice therefore involved a trade-off between detecting severe crashes and limiting false alarms.

#figure(
  image("figures/fig3_logistic_thresholds-1.png", width: 85%),
  caption: [Score Trade-Offs Across Cutoffs. Lowering the cutoff from 0.500 to the validation-best 0.125 trades overall accuracy for higher severe-crash recall and the best F1 score.]
) <fig-lr-thresholds>

