=== Predictor Effects and Limitations

The fitted coefficients were positive for alcohol involvement (+1.06), unlighted darkness (+0.61), and lighted darkness (+0.38), and negative for vehicle count (-1.10), under the selected feature coding. These coefficients indicate associations in the fitted model, not causal effects. Removing vehicle count, alcohol, or lighting reduced validation ROC-AUC by 0.0262, 0.0226, and 0.0187, respectively. Interaction terms produced only a small increase in validation PR-AUC (0.2623 to 0.2644).

#figure(
  image("figures/fig4_logistic_weights_labeled.png", width: 90%),
  caption: [Learned Logistic Regression Weights. Red bars raise severe injury risk (led by alcohol involvement and dark roads); blue bars lower risk (led by multi-car crashes and snow/rain).]
) <fig-lr-weights>

#figure(
  image("figures/fig6_feature_ablation.png", width: 85%),
  caption: [Feature Ablation Impact on Validation ROC-AUC. Removing vehicle count, alcohol, or lighting causes the biggest drops in validation score, while removing time of day gives a tiny gain (+0.0009).]
) <fig-lr-ablation>

The model offers an interpretable baseline but has limited ability to identify severe crashes without substantial false alarms. The analysis used unweighted sampled records, whereas the EDA reported survey-weighted national proportions. Additional predictors and survey-aware evaluation would be needed before drawing national predictive conclusions.

== Tree-Based Models

=== Model Introduction

Two manually defined rule-based classifiers were evaluated to identify crashes with any reported injury. The first model used three variables: number of vehicles, alcohol involvement, and lighting conditions. The second model included six variables by adding weather conditions, crash time, and urban or rural location.

Both models classified crashes as either no apparent injury (MAXSEV_IM = 0) or any injury (MAXSEV_IM = 1–4).

=== Data Preparation and Evaluation

The analysis used 2020 CRSS accident, person, and vehicle records linked by CASENUM. A total of 54,544 crashes with valid injury severity codes were retained. The records were divided into 43,635 reserved development records (80%) and 10,909 test records (20%).

Both models used the same test set. Because the rules were specified in advance, the reserved development records were not used to fit model parameters. These are manually defined rules rather than data-trained decision trees. Performance was evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, and confusion matrices.

=== Model 1: Three Decision Rules

The first model predicted an injury based on three conditions: one vehicle involved, alcohol involvement, or dark but lighted conditions.

#figure(
  image("figures/decision_tree_confusion_3rules_blue.png", width: 85%),
  caption: [Confusion matrix for the three-rule classification model.],
)

The model achieved 57.03% accuracy, 64.40% precision, and 52.68% recall. It correctly identified 3,231 injury crashes but missed 2,902 injury crashes. These results indicate that the model produced relatively fewer false alarms but failed to identify many actual injuries.

=== Model 2: Six Decision Rules

The second model added weather conditions, crash time, and urban or rural location to the original three decision rules.

#figure(
  image("figures/decision_tree_confusion_6rules_blue.png", width: 85%),
  caption: [Confusion matrix for the six-rule classification model.],
)

The model achieved 56.67% accuracy, 59.60% precision, and 71.19% recall. It correctly identified 4,366 injury crashes and missed 1,767. Although its accuracy was slightly lower, the additional rules increased the proportion of injury crashes detected.

