== Neural Network Models

=== Model Introduction

Neural networks were also investigated for predicting crash injury severity. A multilayer perceptron (MLP) was selected because it can identify nonlinear relationships between the input variables and the output. The neural network consists of an input layer, two fully connected hidden layers, and an output layer. The hidden layers contain 64 and 32 neurons, respectively. The ReLU activation function was used, along with a dropout rate of 0.15 to reduce overfitting. The models were trained using cross-entropy loss. Four neural network models were considered in this analysis. Two models use all six selected input groups, with different output classifications. The other two models use separate groups of input variables to investigate their contributions to prediction performance.

=== Data Preparation and Training

For neural-network modeling, 54,544 crashes with severity codes 0–4 were retained. The dataset was divided into three subsets: training, validation, and testing. A stratified split was used to maintain approximately similar injury severity distributions across the subsets.
