=== Summary of Neural Network Findings

Fatal-injury classification remained difficult across the neural-network experiments. Overall accuracy was strongly influenced by the large nonfatal category. Fatal recall and false-positive counts therefore provide necessary context for interpreting the results. The different test splits and the five-class target used for Model 2 limit direct comparisons across all four networks.

= Discussion

== Main Findings

The exploratory analysis identified differences in injury severity across alcohol involvement, lighting conditions, and urban or rural location. In particular, alcohol-involved crashes and crashes in unlighted darkness had higher observed proportions of serious or fatal injuries. These patterns describe associations among sampled crashes and do not establish causal effects.

The predictive experiments addressed different levels of crash injury severity. Logistic regression considered serious or fatal injury versus other outcomes; manually defined rules considered any injury versus no apparent injury; and the neural networks considered fatal versus nonfatal outcomes or all five severity categories. This choice allowed different aspects of the severity problem to be examined. Binary any-injury classification is a broad screening task, whereas fatal-only classification focuses on a rare and important outcome. Five-class classification retains more detail but requires the model to distinguish between similar injury categories.

== Interpretation of Model Performance

Logistic regression showed moderate ability to rank serious or fatal crashes, with a test ROC-AUC of 0.6186. At the validation-selected threshold, 64.36% of serious or fatal crashes were identified, but precision was only 18.99%. The six-rule classifier identified 71.19% of crashes involving any injury, at the cost of more false alarms than the three-rule classifier. These figures answer different classification questions and are not direct measures of relative model quality.

For neural networks, the six-input fatal/nonfatal model achieved 91.6% accuracy but detected only 25.9% of fatal crashes. The five-class network achieved 44.5% accuracy, with low recall for serious and fatal injuries. The separate-input networks reported higher fatal recall in their final test confusion matrices, but their different test record counts prevent a controlled comparison of feature groups. The results do not support choosing a best modeling family solely from the reported accuracies.

The class imbalance is a central limitation. Fatal crashes represent a small fraction of the records, so high accuracy can be obtained even when fatal cases are rarely detected. Precision, recall, balanced accuracy, and confusion matrices are therefore needed in addition to overall accuracy. None of the current results demonstrates sufficient reliability for operational safety decisions.

== Limitations and Future Work

The modeling experiments were limited to the 2020 subset and mostly used six initial predictor groups. Additional information about collision type, travel speed, roadway characteristics, vehicles, and occupants may help distinguish more severe injuries. The EDA used CRSS survey weights for several comparisons, while predictive evaluation was generally based on unweighted sampled records. As a result, the model scores should not be interpreted as nationally representative performance measures.

The models also used different prediction targets and different data splits. A future study should select one common target, use a shared test set, and apply consistent evaluation metrics across logistic regression, learned tree models, and neural networks. Methods for handling class imbalance, tuning classification thresholds, and assessing performance across multiple years should also be evaluated. These steps would provide a stronger basis for selecting a final model.

Overall, the analysis showed that several crash characteristics are associated with injury severity, but reliable prediction of the most severe outcomes remains challenging with the variables and methods considered here.
